## ABSTRACT

The rise of generative artificial intelligence challenges the relationship between human experience and cultural production at its root. This dissertation proposes that the core danger is not technological unemployment but the systemic dilution of perceived emotional authenticity through content hyperproduction, the erosion of the human creative anchor, and the amplification of these effects by algorithmic platform curation. We develop a formal theoretical model, grounded in cultural economics, information theory, and platform studies, that captures the relationship between AI intensity (I), human creative contribution (H), content volume (A), saturation (S), platform amplification (P), and perceived emotional authenticity (E). The model yields a cultural utility function Q(I, H, P) and proves three foundational propositions: the Interior Optimum Theorem (with uniqueness established under concavity conditions), the Authenticity Collapse Threshold, and the Hybrid Stability Condition. The platform amplification factor P is derived from micro-foundations grounded in documented platform recommendation system behavior, not introduced as an ad hoc parameter. A dynamic extension formalizes the evolution of audience authenticity preferences with AI exposure, analyzes steady-state equilibria, and characterizes feasible habituation transition paths. Three simulated scenarios, a formal sensitivity analysis across a (k, P) parameter grid, and robustness checks across (φ, a₁, β) map the optimal AI intensity surface and the safe operating space. All model variables are linked to empirically observable proxies and concrete calibration pathways drawn from eleven validated experimental and observational studies. The results demonstrate that cultural value is maximized not by pure human production nor by full automation, but within a structured hybrid regime whose boundaries depend critically on platform design and evolving audience norms. The dissertation concludes with concrete governance implications — human-creative quotas, mandatory AI disclosure under the EU AI Act's Article 50 framework entering into force in August 2026, Pigouvian saturation taxes calibrated to βP, and algorithmic curation regulation — and identifies the empirical and modeling directions required to transform this framework into a predictive policy instrument.

