## CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE REVIEW

### 2.1 Cultural Economics and the Value of Art

The economics of culture has long recognized that artistic value cannot be reduced to market price. Baumol and Bowen (1966) identified a structural "cost disease" in the performing arts — a tendency for labor-intensive creative industries to lag in productivity growth without a corresponding decline in consumer demand. This foundational insight already gestures at the tension our model will formalize: cultural production is valuable precisely because it is slow, costly, and human. Efficiency is not, in cultural markets, an unambiguous good.

Throsby (2001) formalized this intuition by distinguishing between the economic value and the cultural value of creative goods. Economic value is captured by market price. Cultural value — encompassing aesthetic, symbolic, spiritual, and historical significance — is not. This distinction is central to our project. We treat perceived emotional authenticity as a measurable proxy for Throsby's cultural value, capturing the dimension of creative output most at risk from automated generation. Caves (2000) further characterized creative industries by the "nobody knows" principle — extreme uncertainty about what will resonate emotionally — and by the "art for art's sake" motivation of creative workers. Both features are directly implicated by AI disruption: generative systems reduce uncertainty by averaging across vast training corpora, and they produce output without intrinsic motivation of any kind.

Recent empirical work on AI's labor market effects in creative industries provides important context. A study examining the late 2022 diffusion of modern generative AI as a shock to task content, asking whether artistic occupations more exposed to large language model capabilities experienced different changes in earnings, employment, and hours, finds little evidence so far that generative AI has broadly reduced artists' earnings, suggesting an industry beginning to reorganize around a new technology rather than one experiencing collapse (ACEI, 2026). These findings are consistent with our model's prediction that moderate AI adoption can coexist with human creative labor. The risk lies not in present equilibria but in the trajectory toward high-AI-dominance regimes — the collapse regime formalized in Proposition 2.

### 2.2 Artificial Intelligence and Creative Production: Beyond Displacement

The standard economics literature on AI and labor (Acemoglu & Restrepo, 2018; Eloundou et al., 2023) focuses on task displacement and reinstatement effects. While important, this framing misses the qualitative transformation of cultural output itself. Our concern is not primarily with labor displacement but with the systemic erosion of cultural value when AI-generated content dominates the ecosystem.

#### 2.2.1 Hyperproduction and the Logic of Generative AI

Ferrari and McKelvey (2023) introduce the concept of hyperproduction to theorize the proliferation of AI-generated media as a political-economic phenomenon rooted in rentier capitalism and platformized cultural production. Hyperproduction is not simply more content — it designates a qualitatively new mode of production in which the marginal cost of content approaches zero and the system's imperative becomes the saturation of all available cultural space. The empirical counterpart is documented by Kapwing (2026): AI slop channels on YouTube publish multiple videos per day, far exceeding any human production capacity, and are explicitly designed to optimize engagement metrics rather than cultural value. Our model formalizes hyperproduction through a super-linear content volume function A = a₀ + a₁Iᶠ with φ > 1, capturing the accelerating output dynamics that Ferrari and McKelvey describe.

#### 2.2.2 The Baumol Debate: Curing or Breaking the Cost Disease?

Recent scholarship examines whether generative AI cures the cost disease — by raising productivity in creative sectors — or breaks it by destroying the scarcity that sustained cultural value (Goodridge et al., 2024). Our model provides a formal resolution: AI may cure the cost disease in terms of output quantity, but it creates a new authenticity disease — a collapse of perceived cultural value that more than offsets productivity gains beyond a critical threshold. This answer is not available intuitively; it requires the formal apparatus we develop.

### 2.3 Authenticity and Emotional Response

#### 2.3.1 The Experimental Evidence

The most rigorous evidence on how beliefs about AI authorship shape emotional experience comes from Ponce de Leon and White (2025) at Columbia Business School. Across five studies ranging from art museums to online panels to a corporate lobby, participants saw identical artworks or poems but were told they were created either by a human artist or by an AI system. The findings were consistent: art labeled as AI-generated reliably evokes less awe, and in turn less empathy. The mechanism is cognitive. When you say the art is made by an AI, people don't engage in the same way. They are not searching for deeper meaning. They don't get the aha moment. Human-made art carries traces of intention, effort, and lived experience, and viewers instinctively search for meaning behind the work. When the creator is believed to be an AI system, that reflective process shuts down. This provides the direct experimental foundation for Assumption 2 of our model.

Additional experimental evidence reinforces this finding. Gangadharbatla (2022) found that disclosure of AI authorship significantly reduces perceived creativity and emotional impact of visual artworks, even when the works themselves are unchanged. An eye-tracking study published in Frontiers in Psychology (2025) investigated whether explicit cognitive bias against AI art is accompanied by implicit perceptual mechanisms, using religious art as a notably human domain. An experimental analysis of AI film pitches using a randomized design of 500 participants found that the effects of AI disclosure are domain-specific and context-dependent, with some creative domains showing stronger authenticity penalties than others. Critically, Bellaiche et al. (2024) demonstrated that the negative bias toward AI-generated art is moderated by personality traits, prior experience with AI systems, and anthropocentric beliefs — suggesting that the authenticity decay parameter k in our model is not a universal constant but an aggregate that evolves with the population's AI exposure. This provides the empirical foundation for our dynamic extension.

#### 2.3.2 Meta-Analytic Evidence on AI Creative Capability

At the broader level of creative capability, a meta-analysis of 17 experimental studies published between January 2022 and January 2025, comprising 115 effect sizes comparing human and generative AI performance on creative idea generation tasks, found a small but non-significant pooled effect size favoring generative AI (Organisciak et al., 2025). Sensitivity analysis revealed this was driven by a few studies with very large effects. No significant differences emerged between ideas generated by humans and those produced by prompting GPT-3, GPT-3.5, or GPT-4. Funnel plots, asymmetry tests, and p-curve analysis indicated no publication bias, supporting the findings' validity. Rather than producing genuinely novel, situated ideas, generative AI appears to retrieve statistically likely exemplars of creativity based on training data. This meta-analytic finding is consistent with Assumption 3 of our model: AI does not replace the dimension of human creativity that audiences value; it produces output that is statistically optimized but experientially hollow.

### 2.4 Information Overload, Platform Economics, and Cultural Saturation

A parallel literature in information economics addresses attention scarcity under content abundance. Simon (1971) first noted that a wealth of information creates a poverty of attention. Davenport and Beck (2001) developed this into a full framework for the attention economy. Eriksen (2001) introduced the concept of the tyranny of the moment — the tendency for information overload to flatten cultural time, making all content equally ephemeral and equally forgettable. These insights are directly relevant to our saturation variable S.

#### 2.4.1 Platform Mediation and Algorithmic Curation

The path from content creation to audience attention is mediated by algorithmic curation on platforms including YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, and Facebook. Observational research on platform recommendation systems documents that algorithms rely on measurable engagement signals — watch time, retention, posting frequency, consistency — rather than editorial attributes such as accuracy, sourcing, originality, or narrative structure (Kapwing, 2026). This structural feature creates conditions in which low-quality AI-generated content systematically achieves high visibility, particularly for new accounts where personalization data is limited.

A separate line of empirical research demonstrates that generative AI outperforms humans on precisely the engagement metrics platforms optimize. Across multiple studies using Fortune 500 Twitter data, GPT-4-generated social media posts outperform human-written messages in driving likes, comments, and shares. Even trained public relations professionals, despite explicit instruction and monetary incentives, cannot match AI performance on these metrics (Jungherr & Rauchfleisch, 2024). This creates the fundamental structural incentive problem: the content that performs best on platform metrics is not the content that generates the most cultural value.

Observational evidence on algorithmic amplification further reinforces this point. A study evaluating Twitter's algorithmic amplification used observed digital traces to infer amplification status over a 14-day period, providing evidence that algorithmic systems favor content with certain engagement characteristics regardless of quality (EPJ Data Science, 2023). On TikTok, research on algorithmic amplification dynamics found that content aligned with algorithmic interests undergoes strong amplification within the first 200 videos watched, with a strong negative correlation between algorithmic amplification and genuine audience exploration (arXiv, 2024). These findings directly motivate the platform amplification factor P, which we derive from micro-foundations in Chapter 3.

### 2.5 Gaps in the Existing Literature

Existing work addresses pieces of the problem — labor displacement, aesthetic authenticity, attention scarcity, platform dynamics, and hyperproduction — but no existing model integrates them into a unified formal framework with micro-founded parameters and dynamic extensions. This dissertation fills that gap. The closest antecedent is Cowen's (2002) cultural carrying capacity argument, which holds that markets support cultural diversity up to a threshold beyond which homogenization dominates. We extend this logic into the AI era by adding the dimensions of emotional authenticity, platform amplification with micro-foundations, dynamic audience preferences grounded in empirical evidence, and formal governance analysis tied to live regulation.

