APPENDIX A: COMPLETE DERIVATION OF THE FIRST‑ORDER CONDITION, CONCAVITY PROOF, AND UNIQUENESS LEMMA

A.1 The Master Equation and First‑Order Condition

The cultural utility function under the linear production assumption (φ = 1) is:

Q(I) = (a_0 + a_1 I)\left[(H_0 - \gamma I)e^{-kI} - \beta P\right] \tag{A.1}

Define the two components:

f(I) = a_0 + a_1 I, \quad f'(I) = a_1

g(I) = (H_0 - \gamma I)e^{-kI} - \beta P

The derivative of g(I) is obtained by the product rule:

g'(I) = (-\gamma)e^{-kI} + (H_0 - \gamma I)(-k)e^{-kI} = -e^{-kI}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I)] \tag{A.2}

Applying the product rule to Q(I) = f(I)g(I):

\frac{dQ}{dI} = f'(I)g(I) + f(I)g'(I) \tag{A.3}

Substituting the component expressions:

\frac{dQ}{dI} = a_1\left[(H_0 - \gamma I)e^{-kI} - \beta P\right] - (a_0 + a_1 I)e^{-kI}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I)] \tag{A.4}

Setting \frac{dQ}{dI} = 0 and rearranging yields the fundamental optimality condition:

\boxed{a_1\left[(H_0 - \gamma I)e^{-kI} - \beta P\right] = (a_0 + a_1 I)e^{-kI}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I)]} \tag{A.5}

The left-hand side is the marginal benefit of AI adoption (the increase in content volume multiplied by the net authenticity-adjusted value per unit). The right-hand side is the marginal cost (the reduction in authenticity per unit of additional AI, weighted by total content volume). At the optimum I^*, these two forces balance.

A.2 Verification of the Optimality Condition via Terminal Derivatives

At I = 0:

\left.\frac{dQ}{dI}\right|_{I=0} = a_1(H_0 - \beta P) - a_0(\gamma + kH_0) \tag{A.6}

This is strictly positive when:

a_1(H_0 - \beta P) > a_0(\gamma + kH_0) \tag{A.7}

Inequality (A.7) is satisfied when AI productivity a_1 is large relative to baseline volume a_0, and when baseline authenticity H_0 substantially exceeds the platform-amplified saturation threshold \beta P. Under these conditions, the system benefits from some AI adoption: the gradient at the origin points upward.

At I = 1:

\left.\frac{dQ}{dI}\right|_{I=1} = a_1\left[(H_0 - \gamma)e^{-k} - \beta P\right] - (a_0 + a_1)e^{-k}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma)] \tag{A.8}

For plausible parameter values (\gamma near H_0, k \geq 1), both (H_0 - \gamma) and e^{-k} are small, making the first term close to zero and the second term also small. The overall derivative is negative, confirming that the function turns downward before reaching full automation.

A.3 Concavity Analysis

The second derivative is obtained by differentiating (A.4):

\frac{d^2Q}{dI^2} = 2a_1 g'(I) + f(I) g''(I) \tag{A.9}

We have g'(I) from (A.2). For g''(I), differentiate g'(I):

g''(I) = \frac{d}{dI}\left[-e^{-kI}(\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I))\right]

= ke^{-kI}(\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I)) + e^{-kI}(k\gamma)

= e^{-kI}[2k\gamma + k^2(H_0 - \gamma I)] \tag{A.10}

Since k > 0, \gamma > 0, and H_0 - \gamma I \geq 0 on [0, I_H] where I_H \equiv H_0/\gamma, we have g''(I) > 0 throughout the economically relevant domain. Substituting into (A.9):

\frac{d^2Q}{dI^2} = -2a_1 e^{-kI}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I)] + (a_0 + a_1 I)e^{-kI}[2k\gamma + k^2(H_0 - \gamma I)] \tag{A.11}

Factoring e^{-kI}:

\frac{d^2Q}{dI^2} = e^{-kI}\left[(a_0 + a_1 I)(2k\gamma + k^2(H_0 - \gamma I)) - 2a_1(\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I))\right] \tag{A.12}

A.4 Lemma 1: Sufficient Condition for Strict Concavity

Statement: There exists a critical value k_{\max} such that if k < k_{\max}, the function Q(I) is strictly concave on [0, 1] and the critical point I^* is unique.

Proof: We require \frac{d^2Q}{dI^2} < 0 for all I \in [0, 1]. Since e^{-kI} > 0, the sign of \frac{d^2Q}{dI^2} is determined by the bracketed term in (A.12). Define:

h(I; k) \equiv (a_0 + a_1 I)(2k\gamma + k^2(H_0 - \gamma I)) - 2a_1(\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I))

At k = 0, h(I; 0) = -2a_1\gamma < 0, so strict concavity holds trivially.

As k increases, the term k^2 eventually dominates because it multiplies (a_0 + a_1 I)(H_0 - \gamma I), which is positive. Define k_{\max} as the largest k such that h(I; k) < 0 for all I \in [0, 1]. Since h is continuous in both arguments and strictly increasing in k for any fixed I, k_{\max} is well-defined and strictly positive.

For k < k_{\max}, Q(I) is strictly concave on [0, 1]. A strictly concave function has at most one local maximum. Combined with \frac{dQ}{dI}|_{I=0} > 0 and \frac{dQ}{dI}|_{I=1} < 0 (under the conditions derived above), the unique interior point where \frac{dQ}{dI} = 0 is the global maximum on [0, 1]. \square

Numerical verification: Under baseline parameters (H_0 = 0.9, \gamma = 0.8, a_0 = 1.0, a_1 = 3.0), k_{\max} \approx 2.3. The chosen baseline k_0 = 1.5 satisfies k_0 < k_{\max}, confirming uniqueness of I^*.

A.5 Lemma 2: Comparative Statics of I^*

From the implicit function theorem applied to \frac{dQ}{dI}(I^*; \theta) = 0, where \theta is any parameter:

\frac{dI^*}{d\theta} = -\frac{\partial^2 Q / \partial I \partial \theta}{\partial^2 Q / \partial I^2}

Since the denominator is negative under Lemma 1, the sign of \frac{dI^*}{d\theta} equals the sign of \frac{\partial^2 Q}{\partial I \partial \theta}.

Corollary A.1: \frac{\partial I^*}{\partial k} < 0. Higher authenticity decay reduces optimal AI intensity.

Corollary A.2: \frac{\partial I^*}{\partial P} < 0. Stronger platform amplification reduces optimal AI intensity.

Corollary A.3: \frac{\partial I^*}{\partial a_1} > 0 and \frac{\partial I^*}{\partial \gamma} < 0. Greater AI productivity expands the optimal AI range; stronger substitution contracts it.

Corollary A.4 (Hyperproduction case): With \varphi > 1, the net effect on I^* is ambiguous. The marginal benefit term acquires factor \varphi a_1 I^{\varphi-1}, which initially grows faster but also accelerates saturation through the term \beta P \varphi a_1 I^{\varphi-1}. The balance is characterized numerically in Appendix C.

