APPENDIX B: THE AUTHENTICITY COLLAPSE THRESHOLD — DERIVATION, COMPARATIVE STATICS, AND ABSORBING PROPERTY

B.1 Definition and Derivation of I_c

The collapse threshold I_c is defined as the smallest AI intensity at which cultural utility becomes non-positive:

I_c \equiv \min\{I \in [0, 1] : Q(I) = 0\}

From the master equation (A.1), Q(I) = 0 when:

(H_0 - \gamma I)e^{-kI} = \beta P \tag{B.1}

This transcendental equation does not admit a closed-form solution in general but is solved numerically for any given parameter vector. For the linear baseline:

Numerical example: Under baseline parameters (H_0 = 0.9, \gamma = 0.8, k = 1.5, \beta = 0.2, P = 2.0), solving (B.1) yields I_c \approx 0.74.

B.2 Comparative Statics of I_c

Applying the implicit function theorem to (B.1):

F(I_c; \theta) \equiv (H_0 - \gamma I_c)e^{-k I_c} - \beta P = 0

The partial derivatives are:

\frac{\partial F}{\partial I_c} = -\gamma e^{-k I_c} - k(H_0 - \gamma I_c)e^{-k I_c} = -e^{-k I_c}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I_c)] < 0

\frac{\partial F}{\partial P} = -\beta < 0, \quad \frac{\partial F}{\partial k} = -I_c(H_0 - \gamma I_c)e^{-k I_c} < 0, \quad \frac{\partial F}{\partial \beta} = -P < 0

\frac{\partial F}{\partial \gamma} = -I_c e^{-k I_c} < 0, \quad \frac{\partial F}{\partial H_0} = e^{-k I_c} > 0

Hence:

\frac{d I_c}{d P} = -\frac{\partial F / \partial P}{\partial F / \partial I_c} = -\frac{-\beta}{-e^{-k I_c}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I_c)]} < 0

\frac{d I_c}{d k} < 0, \quad \frac{d I_c}{d \beta} < 0, \quad \frac{d I_c}{d \gamma} < 0, \quad \frac{d I_c}{d H_0} > 0

Key policy implication: \frac{d I_c}{d P} < 0 is of central importance. Higher platform amplification brings the collapse threshold closer, narrowing the safe operating space. Reducing P — through regulation of algorithmic curation — expands the range of AI intensity that the cultural ecosystem can sustain without entering net destruction.

B.3 Proposition: The Collapse Regime Is Absorbing

Statement: For any I > I_c, Q(I) < 0 and \frac{dQ}{dI} < 0. Once the system enters the collapse regime, marginal reductions in I restore utility only up to Q(I_c) = 0, and no spontaneous recovery is possible without external intervention that shifts the parameter environment.

Proof: For I > I_c, by definition of I_c, we have (H_0 - \gamma I)e^{-kI} < \beta P. Hence E - \beta P < 0, and:

Q(I) = (a_0 + a_1 I^{\varphi})(E - \beta P) < 0

For the derivative:

\frac{dQ}{dI} = \varphi a_1 I^{\varphi-1}(E - \beta P) + (a_0 + a_1 I^{\varphi}) \cdot \frac{dE}{dI}

Since E - \beta P < 0 and \frac{dE}{dI} = -e^{-kI}[\gamma + k(H_0 - \gamma I)] < 0, both terms are strictly negative. Hence \frac{dQ}{dI} < 0 for all I > I_c.

Absorbing property: The function Q(I) is continuous on [0, 1] with Q(I_c) = 0 and Q(I) < 0 for I > I_c. For any \varepsilon > 0, Q(I_c + \varepsilon) < 0. Reducing I from I_c + \varepsilon toward I_c raises Q toward zero, but only reaches Q = 0 at exactly I = I_c. There is no discontinuous recovery: the collapse is entered continuously, and exit requires reducing AI intensity below I_c. Moreover, if the system has been in the collapse regime and audience trust has eroded beyond the structural parameter k, the true collapse threshold may be lower than the static I_c, making recovery more difficult than the static analysis suggests. \square

B.4 Collapse Threshold with Hyperproduction (\varphi > 1)

With A = a_0 + a_1 I^{\varphi}, the collapse condition remains (B.1) because Q(I) = 0 when E - \beta P = 0, independent of the content volume function. However, the severity of collapse differs:

Q(I) = (a_0 + a_1 I^{\varphi})(E - \beta P)

For I > I_c, the magnitude of negative utility scales with I^{\varphi} rather than I. Hyperproduction (\varphi > 1) therefore deepens the collapse: the cultural destruction per unit of AI intensity beyond the threshold is larger than under the linear model. This formalizes the heightened danger that the hyperproduction framework (Ferrari & McKelvey, 2023) identifies.

