APPENDIX E: MICRO-FOUNDATIONS FOR THE PLATFORM AMPLIFICATION FACTOR P

E.1 Platform Model Setup

Consider a platform (e.g., YouTube, TikTok, Instagram) that ranks content using an algorithmic recommendation system. Following the Kapwing (2026) analysis, the platform's recommendation system relies on measurable engagement signals—watch time, retention, posting frequency, consistency—while editorial attributes such as accuracy, sourcing, originality, and narrative structure do not directly factor into ranking.

Let there be two content types: human-crafted (H-type) with volume A_H, and AI-generated (A-type) with volume A_A. Total content volume is A = A_H + A_A.

Define AI intensity as I \equiv A_A / A. Then A_A = I A and A_H = (1 - I) A.

E.2 Engagement Advantage of AI-Generated Content

Empirical evidence demonstrates that AI-generated content systematically outperforms human-written content on engagement metrics. Jungherr and Rauchfleisch (2024) provide multi-study evidence that GPT-4-generated social media posts outperform human-written messages in driving likes, comments, and shares. Let the per-unit engagement generated by A-type content exceed that of H-type content by a factor \rho > 1:

\text{Engagement per unit: } e_A = \rho \cdot e_H, \quad \rho > 1

Empirically, \rho can be estimated from A/B testing with matched content pairs or from observational data comparing engagement rates on AI-attributed versus human-attributed content.

E.3 Platform Optimization and Amplification

The platform's recommendation algorithm, optimizing for aggregate user engagement, allocates visibility proportionally to expected engagement. The visibility share of A-type content is:

\text{Visibility}_A = \frac{e_A A_A}{e_A A_A + e_H A_H} = \frac{\rho I A}{\rho I A + (1 - I) A} = \frac{\rho I}{\rho I + (1 - I)} \tag{E.1}

The raw volume share of A-type content is simply I. Define the amplification factor \omega as the degree to which algorithmic allocation exceeds proportional representation:

\omega \equiv \frac{\text{Visibility}_A / I}{\text{Visibility}_H / (1 - I)} - 1 = \rho - 1 \tag{E.2}

E.4 Effective Saturation Function

The saturation burden depends on effective visibility, not raw volume. Effective saturation is:

S = \beta \cdot (\text{Effective Content Volume}) = \beta \cdot \left(A_H + (1 + \omega) A_A\right)

Substituting A_A = I A and A_H = (1 - I) A:

S = \beta A \left[(1 - I) + (1 + \omega) I\right] = \beta A [1 + \omega I]

Define P \equiv 1 + \omega I. Under the simplifying assumption that \omega is approximately constant over the relevant range (i.e., the engagement advantage \rho is stable), P captures the platform amplification effect. For tractability in the aggregate model, we treat P as a policy-relevant structural parameter that can be influenced by curation regulation.

\boxed{S = \beta P A, \quad P = 1 + \omega I \approx 1 + (\rho - 1) I}

E.5 Calibration Sources for P

The parameter \rho is estimable from engagement data. Kapwing (2026) provides the content analysis framework; the Jungherr and Rauchfleisch (2024) multi-study design provides the experimental methodology. Observational amplification studies—such as the Corsi (2024) analysis of Twitter algorithmic amplification in EPJ Data Science, and the arXiv (2024) study of TikTok amplification dynamics—provide methods for estimating \omega and hence P from digital trace data.

The derivation transforms P from an ad hoc parameter into a quantity with clear structural interpretation: P = 1 + (\rho - 1)I, where \rho is the empirically documented engagement advantage of AI-generated content and I is the AI intensity. As \rho or I increases, platform amplification strengthens, narrowing the safe operating space and reducing the optimal AI intensity—as formalized in Propositions 1 and 2 and quantified in the sensitivity tables of Appendix C.